\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For each fixed $\varepsilon >0$, is approximating the minimum vertex cover of a finite simple unweighted graph within $2-\varepsilon $ NP-hard under deterministic polynomial-time reductions? No bounded-degree or counting-logic indistinguishability promise is imposed.

\subsection{Short English statement}
For each fixed $\varepsilon >0$, is approximating the minimum vertex cover of a finite simple unweighted graph within $2-\varepsilon $ NP-hard under deterministic polynomial-time reductions? No bounded-degree or counting-logic indistinguishability promise is imposed.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] Original question source or mathematical context.
\url{http://www.openproblemgarden.org/op/vertex_cover_integrality_gap}.
\item[S2] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026/README.md}.
\end{itemize}

