\section*{Factor-two hardness of Vertex Cover}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 102; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that every fixed approximation factor strictly below two for Vertex Cover is NP-hard on simple unweighted graphs.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 102; 2026-09-23), compatible local-answer tables become graph vertices, with edges encoding failed consistency. An unusually large independent set gives conditional gradients and bounded label lists; pair decoding contradicts the source gap, while satisfiable inputs provide the matching independent-set density.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The hardness statement concerns simple unweighted graphs and every fixed approximation factor below two. This catalogue does not establish complete coverage of the exact reduction claim in this record and retains a partial label.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 102; 2026-09-23), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: every fixed approximation factor strictly below two for Vertex Cover is NP-hard on simple unweighted graphs.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{DirectedFeedback.lean}, \texttt{MinUncut.lean}, \texttt{OptimalMaxCut.lean}, \texttt{UniqueGamesTheorem.lean}, \texttt{VertexCover.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.DirectedFeedback.main}, \texttt{OAI.MinUncut.main}, \texttt{OAI.OptimalMaxCut.main}, \texttt{OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.theorem11}, \texttt{OAI.VertexCover.every\_fixed\_factor\_below\_two}, \texttt{OAI.VertexCover.explicit\_cover\_gap}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

